Hi Stijn,

Here's the patch. The explanation (and the patch) below is for our
earlier work on determinism. For our latest work on race detection we
did resolve even races denoted with NODATARACE_OPTIONAL (most of them
can be resolve without additional synchronization).

-- Cedomir

---------- Forwarded message ----------
From: Cedomir Segulja <seguljac@eecg.toronto.edu>
Date: Wed, Sep 17, 2014 at 6:50 PM
Subject: Re: Benchmarks in Cost of Weak Determinism PACT paper
To: Gabriel Southern <gsouther@soe.ucsc.edu>
Cc: Abdelrahman Tarek <tsa@eecg.toronto.edu>, Jose Renau <renau@soe.ucsc.edu>


Hi Gabriel,

I'm sending you the patch of the changes we did to the parsec 3.0
suite (originally downloaded from
http://parsec.cs.princeton.edu/download/3.0/parsec-3.0-core.tar.gz).
To apply the patch please copy it to the parsec-3.0 directory and from
there do "patch -p1 < parsec-3.0-drf.patch".

There are two types of changes:

1) Changes to make the code data-race-free:

These are denoted with the NODATARACE flag. To help understand why
these changes were made, I am also attaching an Excel sheet, which is
in a rough shape but it should provide some more info. Some of these
changes (noted with NODATARACE_OPTIONAL) we did not enable during our
experiments. Although the NODATARACE_OPTIONAL flag also resolves data
races, these data races did not cause any nondeterminism (e.g., racy
write of the same value) and they could be potentially resolved
without introducing additional synchronization.

2) Other small changes. These include for example:
- changes to make benchmarks compatible with our infrastructure (e.g.,
always join threads before exiting, use mutex instead spin lock
pthread api so we can properly intercept it, use native pthread barriers, etc.)
- bug fixes

We are currently in the process of writing a short paper that
discusses these data races, and the performance impact of removing
them. That will more concisely explain the data races we identified
and how we removed them. In the meantime, if you find this patch
useful, please cite our PACT paper.

Please let me know if you have any questions or comments.

Cheers,
-- Cedomir

